 **Second fetch.** The provider completes some player stats days after the match, so a finished fixture whose latest fetch came under 3 days (72 hours) after kickoff is fetched once more when it is 3 days old, in every competition, idle ones included (`coverage.second_fetch_due`). From the moment that fetch is due until it is made, the fixture's player stats count as not covered. A fixture whose latest fetch is in the provider's newer player-stats format (players listed, none with goals conceded) and came under 14 days after kickoff is fetched once more when it is 14 days old (`coverage.newer_format_refetch_due`): that format never carries goals conceded or dribbled past, and the provider replaces it with the complete one for many matches later. The provider adds player and team stats to some matches weeks after kickoff. So a fixture with no player rows or no statistics in any fetch is fetched again at 7 and at 30 days old (`coverage.late_fetch_due`). Each of those fetches stays due for 7 days. An older gap is filled only by `scripts/refetch_from_provider.py`.
 
-**Both versions are kept deliberately, and base picks.** The bundle carries lineups, events, statistics and player stats *together*, so a retry chasing late statistics can come back richer in one section and poorer in another. `base_apif__fixture_statistics` takes each stat of a team line from the newest fetch that carries it. A card count is taken only as the cleaning rules' "Fetches" line allows. `base_apif__fixture_players` takes a match's player rows from one fetch, its newest fetch in the complete format (any player with goals conceded), else its newest fetch. The "Fetches" line says where a player's value comes from another fetch or from the events instead. `base_apif__coaches` takes each bio field from the newest fetch that carries it. `base_apif__fixture_events` keeps the event list of the fixture's latest fetch that has events: positions past the end of a shorter later list are not kept.
